1.1 Algorithm and Its Properties
What is an Algorithm?

An algorithm is a finite sequence of well-defined steps to solve a problem or perform a computation.

📌 Example: Steps to calculate factorial of a number.

Properties (Characteristics) of an Algorithm

An algorithm must satisfy the following:

Input – Takes zero or more inputs

Output – Produces at least one output

Definiteness – Each step is clear and unambiguous

Finiteness – Terminates after finite steps

Effectiveness – Steps are basic and feasible

Correctness – Gives correct output for all valid inputs

👉 These are often asked in exams.

RAM Model (Random Access Machine Model)

The RAM model is a theoretical model used to analyze algorithm performance.

Assumptions of RAM Model

Each instruction takes constant time

Memory access takes constant time

Uses simple operations: +, −, ×, /, comparison

📌 Helps us count number of operations, not actual execution time.

Time Complexity
Definition

Time complexity is the amount of time an algorithm takes as a function of input size n.

We measure:

Best Case

Average Case

Worst Case (most important for exams)

📌 Expressed using asymptotic notations (O, Ω, Θ).

Space Complexity
Definition

Space complexity is the total memory used by an algorithm.

Includes:

Fixed space (constants, variables)

Variable space (arrays, recursion stack)

Detailed Analysis of Algorithm (Factorial Example)
Factorial Algorithm (Iterative)
FACT(n)
1. result ← 1
2. for i = 1 to n
3.     result ← result × i
4. return result

Time Complexity Analysis

Loop runs n times

Each operation is constant time

👉 Time Complexity = O(n)

Space Complexity

Uses only constant variables

👉 Space Complexity = O(1)

Concept of Aggregate Analysis
What is Aggregate Analysis?

Aggregate analysis finds the average cost per operation over a sequence of operations without considering probabilistic input.

Example

If total cost of n operations is n²
👉 Average cost per operation = n² / n = n

📌 Used in amortized analysis (e.g., stack operations).

1.2 Asymptotic Notations

Asymptotic notations describe growth rate of functions as input size becomes large.

Big-O Notation (O)
Definition

Big-O gives the upper bound (worst-case complexity).

If
f(n) ≤ c × g(n) for all n ≥ n₀

👉 Then f(n) = O(g(n))

Example
f(n) = 3n + 5
O(n)

Big-Omega Notation (Ω)
Definition

Big-Ω gives the lower bound (best-case complexity).

If
f(n) ≥ c × g(n) for all n ≥ n₀

👉 Then f(n) = Ω(g(n))

Example
f(n) = 3n + 5
Ω(n)

Big-Theta Notation (Θ)
Definition

Big-Θ gives the tight bound (both upper and lower).

If
c₁g(n) ≤ f(n) ≤ c₂g(n)

👉 Then f(n) = Θ(g(n))

Example
f(n) = 3n + 5
Θ(n)

Geometrical Interpretation
Notation	Meaning
O(g(n))	f(n) lies below g(n)
Ω(g(n))	f(n) lies above g(n)
Θ(g(n))	f(n) lies between two curves

📌 Often asked as theory + diagram question.

1.3 Recurrences
Recursive Algorithms

A recursive algorithm solves a problem by calling itself on smaller inputs.

Example: Factorial (Recursive)
fact(n):
if n == 0 return 1
return n × fact(n−1)

Recurrence Relation

A recurrence relation expresses time complexity of a recursive algorithm.

📌 General form:

T(n) = aT(n/b) + f(n)

Solving Recurrences
1. Recursion Tree Method

Break the recurrence into a tree of subproblems.

Example
T(n) = 2T(n/2) + n


Level 0 cost: n

Level 1 cost: n

Level 2 cost: n

Height: log n

👉 Total Cost = n log n

2. Substitution Method

Guess the solution

Prove by mathematical induction

Example
T(n) = 2T(n/2) + n
Assume T(n) = O(n log n)


Prove by substitution.

📌 Used mostly in theoretical questions.

3. Master’s Theorem

Used to solve recurrences of the form:

T(n) = aT(n/b) + f(n)

Three Cases
Case	Condition	Result
Case 1	f(n) < n^(logb a)	Θ(n^(logb a))
Case 2	f(n) = n^(logb a)	Θ(n^(logb a) log n)
Case 3	f(n) > n^(logb a)	Θ(f(n))
Example
T(n) = 2T(n/2) + n


a = 2

b = 2

f(n) = n

log₂2 = 1

👉 Case 2

✔ T(n) = Θ(n log n)

