2.1 Basic Algorithms
1️⃣ Algorithm for GCD (Greatest Common Divisor)
What is GCD?

GCD of two numbers is the largest number that divides both numbers.

📌 Example:
GCD of 12 and 18 = 6

Euclidean Algorithm (Iterative)

Idea:
Keep dividing until the remainder becomes 0.

Algorithm
GCD(a, b)
while b ≠ 0
    r = a % b
    a = b
    b = r
return a

Time Complexity

Each step reduces the number

Runs at most log n times

👉 Time Complexity: O(log n)

Space Complexity

Uses only variables

👉 Space Complexity: O(1)

2️⃣ Fibonacci Number (Iterative)
What is Fibonacci Series?

Each number is the sum of previous two numbers.

📌 Series:
0, 1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 13 …

Iterative Algorithm
Fibonacci(n)
if n == 0 return 0
if n == 1 return 1

a = 0, b = 1
for i = 2 to n
    c = a + b
    a = b
    b = c
return b

Time Complexity

Loop runs n times

👉 Time Complexity: O(n)

Space Complexity

Uses only 3 variables

👉 Space Complexity: O(1)

2.2 Searching Algorithm
Sequential Search (Linear Search)
What is Searching?

Finding an element in a list.

Sequential Search

Check elements one by one from start to end.

📌 Works on sorted and unsorted arrays.

Algorithm
LinearSearch(A, n, key)
for i = 1 to n
    if A[i] == key
        return i
return -1

Time Complexity
Case	Time
Best Case	O(1)
Worst Case	O(n)
Average Case	O(n)

👉 Overall: O(n)

Space Complexity

No extra memory used

👉 O(1)

2.3 Sorting Algorithms
What is Sorting?

Arranging elements in ascending or descending order.

📌 Example:
Before: 5, 2, 4, 1
After: 1, 2, 4, 5

1️⃣ Bubble Sort
Idea

Compare adjacent elements

Swap if they are in wrong order

Biggest element bubbles up to the end

Algorithm
BubbleSort(A, n)
for i = 1 to n-1
    for j = 1 to n-i
        if A[j] > A[j+1]
            swap(A[j], A[j+1])

Time Complexity
Case	Time
Best	O(n)
Worst	O(n²)
Average	O(n²)
Space Complexity

👉 O(1) (in-place)

Advantages

✔ Very easy to understand
✔ Simple implementation

Disadvantages

❌ Very slow for large data

2️⃣ Selection Sort
Idea

Find smallest element

Put it at the beginning

Repeat for remaining list

Algorithm
SelectionSort(A, n)
for i = 1 to n-1
    min = i
    for j = i+1 to n
        if A[j] < A[min]
            min = j
    swap(A[i], A[min])

Time Complexity
Case	Time
Best	O(n²)
Worst	O(n²)
Average	O(n²)
Space Complexity

👉 O(1)

Advantages

✔ Fewer swaps
✔ Simple logic

Disadvantages

❌ Always slow (O(n²))

3️⃣ Insertion Sort
Idea

Like arranging playing cards

Insert each element in its correct position

Algorithm
InsertionSort(A, n)
for i = 2 to n
    key = A[i]
    j = i - 1
    while j ≥ 1 and A[j] > key
        A[j+1] = A[j]
        j = j - 1
    A[j+1] = key

Time Complexity
Case	Time
Best (sorted list)	O(n)
Worst	O(n²)
Average	O(n²)
Space Complexity

👉 O(1)

Advantages

✔ Fast for small or sorted data
✔ Stable sorting

Disadvantages

❌ Slow for large data

📌 COMPARISON TABLE (VERY IMPORTANT)
Algorithm	Best	Worst	Space
Bubble	O(n)	O(n²)	O(1)
Selection	O(n²)	O(n²)	O(1)
Insertion	O(n)	O(n²)	O(1)
EXAM TIPS

✔ Write algorithm steps clearly
✔ Mention time & space complexity
✔ Use simple examples
✔ Comparison table = high marks


