UNIT 3: DIVIDE AND CONQUER ALGORITHMS (8 Hrs)
What is Divide and Conquer?

Divide and Conquer is a technique where a problem is solved in three steps:

Divide – Break the problem into smaller sub-problems

Conquer – Solve each sub-problem

Combine – Combine results to get the final answer

📌 Example: Merge Sort, Quick Sort, Binary Search

3.1 Searching Algorithms
1️⃣ Binary Search
Idea

Works only on a sorted array

Compare middle element with key

Reduce search space by half

Algorithm
BinarySearch(A, low, high, key)
if low > high
    return -1

mid = (low + high) / 2

if A[mid] == key
    return mid
else if key < A[mid]
    return BinarySearch(A, low, mid-1, key)
else
    return BinarySearch(A, mid+1, high, key)

Time Complexity
Case	Time
Best	O(1)
Worst	O(log n)
Average	O(log n)
Space Complexity

Recursive version: O(log n)

Iterative version: O(1)

2️⃣ Min-Max Finding (Divide & Conquer)
Problem

Find minimum and maximum element in an array using fewer comparisons.

Idea

Divide array into two halves

Find min & max of each half

Compare results

Algorithm Idea
MinMax(A, low, high)
if low == high
    min = max = A[low]
else if high == low + 1
    compare A[low] and A[high]
else
    mid = (low + high) / 2
    (min1, max1) = MinMax(A, low, mid)
    (min2, max2) = MinMax(A, mid+1, high)

    min = smaller(min1, min2)
    max = larger(max1, max2)

Time Complexity

Number of comparisons ≈ 3n/2 – 2

👉 Time Complexity: O(n)

Space Complexity

👉 O(log n) (recursion)

3.2 Sorting Algorithms
1️⃣ Merge Sort
Idea

Divide array into halves

Sort each half

Merge sorted halves

Algorithm
MergeSort(A, l, r)
if l < r
    mid = (l + r) / 2
    MergeSort(A, l, mid)
    MergeSort(A, mid+1, r)
    Merge(A, l, mid, r)

Time Complexity
Case	Time
Best	O(n log n)
Worst	O(n log n)
Average	O(n log n)
Space Complexity

👉 O(n) (extra array)

Advantages

✔ Very efficient
✔ Stable sorting

Disadvantages

❌ Uses extra memory

2️⃣ Quick Sort
Idea

Choose a pivot

Place pivot at correct position

Elements smaller → left, larger → right

Algorithm
QuickSort(A, low, high)
if low < high
    p = Partition(A, low, high)
    QuickSort(A, low, p-1)
    QuickSort(A, p+1, high)

Time Complexity
Case	Time
Best	O(n log n)
Average	O(n log n)
Worst	O(n²)

📌 Worst case occurs when pivot is smallest or largest element.

Space Complexity

👉 O(log n) (average)

Advantages

✔ Very fast in practice
✔ In-place sorting

Disadvantages

❌ Worst-case is slow

3️⃣ Heap Sort
Heap

A heap is a complete binary tree where:

Max Heap: parent ≥ children

Min Heap: parent ≤ children

Heapify
Idea

Fix heap property at a node.

Heapify(A, n, i)
largest = i
l = 2i
r = 2i + 1

if l ≤ n and A[l] > A[largest]
    largest = l
if r ≤ n and A[r] > A[largest]
    largest = r

if largest ≠ i
    swap(A[i], A[largest])
    Heapify(A, n, largest)

Build Heap

Call Heapify from bottom nodes

Converts array into heap

👉 Time: O(n)

Heap Sort Algorithm

Build max heap

Swap first and last element

Reduce heap size

Heapify again

Time Complexity
Case	Time
Best	O(n log n)
Worst	O(n log n)
Average	O(n log n)
Space Complexity

👉 O(1)

4️⃣ Randomized Quick Sort
Idea

Choose pivot randomly

Reduces chance of worst case

Why Randomization?

Prevents bad input order.

Time Complexity
Case	Time
Best	O(n log n)
Expected	O(n log n)
Worst	O(n²) (rare)
3.3 Order Statistics
What is Order Statistic?

Finding the k-th smallest or largest element.

📌 Example:

1st smallest = minimum

n/2-th smallest = median

1️⃣ Selection in Expected Linear Time (Randomized Select)
Idea

Based on Quick Sort

Uses random pivot

Only explores one side

Time Complexity

👉 Expected O(n)

Why Fast?

Random pivot avoids worst case most of the time

2️⃣ Selection in Worst Case Linear Time (Median of Medians)
Idea

Divide elements into groups of 5

Find median of each group

Find median of medians

Use it as pivot

Guarantee

✔ Balanced partition always

Time Complexity

👉 Worst Case O(n)

Why Important?

Gives guaranteed performance

Used in theoretical analysis

📌 IMPORTANT COMPARISON TABLE
Algorithm	Best	Worst	Space
Merge Sort	n log n	n log n	O(n)
Quick Sort	n log n	n²	O(log n)
Heap Sort	n log n	n log n	O(1)
Randomized QS	n log n	n²	O(log n)
EXAM TIPS

✔ Divide & Conquer steps
✔ Binary Search needs sorted array
✔ Merge vs Quick vs Heap comparison
✔ Randomized Quick Sort advantage
✔ Order statistics definitions
