UNIT 4: GREEDY ALGORITHMS (6 Hrs)
What is a Greedy Algorithm?

A greedy algorithm solves a problem by:
👉 Choosing the best option at each step, hoping this leads to the best final solution.

📌 It never goes back to change decisions.

4.1 Optimization Problems
What is an Optimization Problem?

An optimization problem is a problem where we need to:
✔ Maximize profit / value
✔ Minimize cost / time / distance

Optimal Solution

The best possible solution among all valid solutions.

Introduction to Greedy Algorithms

Greedy algorithms:

Work step by step

Make locally optimal choice

Fast and easy to implement

📌 Not all problems can be solved using greedy method.

Elements of Greedy Strategy

A greedy algorithm has these parts:

Candidate Set – all possible choices

Selection Function – chooses best candidate

Feasibility Function – checks if choice is allowed

Objective Function – measures quality (profit, cost)

Solution Function – stops when solution is complete

4.2 Greedy Algorithms
1️⃣ Fractional Knapsack
Problem

You have:

Items with weight and profit

Knapsack with fixed capacity

You can take fraction of an item

Idea

Choose items with highest profit/weight ratio first.

Algorithm
FractionalKnapsack(items, W)
Sort items by profit/weight (descending)

for each item
    if item.weight ≤ W
        take whole item
        W = W - item.weight
    else
        take fraction of item
        break

Time Complexity

👉 O(n log n) (due to sorting)

Space Complexity

👉 O(1)

Important Note

✔ Greedy works here
❌ Does NOT work for 0/1 Knapsack

2️⃣ Job Sequencing with Deadlines
Problem

Jobs have profit and deadline

Each job takes 1 unit time

Only one job can be done at a time

Maximize total profit

Idea

Sort jobs by profit (descending)

Schedule job as late as possible before deadline

Algorithm (Idea)
Sort jobs by profit
for each job
    for time = deadline to 1
        if slot is free
            schedule job
            break

Time Complexity

👉 O(n²)

Space Complexity

👉 O(n)

3️⃣ Kruskal’s Algorithm
Problem

Find Minimum Spanning Tree (MST).

📌 MST connects all vertices with:
✔ Minimum total weight
✔ No cycle

Idea

Sort edges by weight

Add smallest edge

Avoid cycle

Algorithm
Sort all edges by weight
for each edge
    if adding edge does not form cycle
        add edge to MST

Time Complexity

👉 O(E log E)

Space Complexity

👉 O(V)

4️⃣ Prim’s Algorithm
Problem

Find Minimum Spanning Tree.

Idea

Start from any node

Always choose minimum weight edge that connects tree to new vertex

Algorithm (Simple Idea)
Start with any vertex
while all vertices not included
    choose smallest edge connecting tree to outside vertex

Time Complexity
Implementation	Time
Adjacency Matrix	O(V²)
Priority Queue	O(E log V)
Space Complexity

👉 O(V)

Kruskal vs Prim
Kruskal	Prim
Edge based	Vertex based
Best for sparse graph	Best for dense graph
5️⃣ Dijkstra’s Algorithm
Problem

Find shortest path from source to all vertices.

📌 Works only with non-negative weights.

Idea

Start from source

Always pick closest unvisited vertex

Relax edges

Algorithm (Idea)
Initialize distances from source
repeat
    select vertex with minimum distance
    update distances of its neighbors
until all vertices visited

Time Complexity
Implementation	Time
Simple array	O(V²)
Min Heap	O(E log V)
Space Complexity

👉 O(V)

4.3 Huffman Coding
Purpose of Huffman Coding

Used for:
✔ Data compression
✔ Reducing file size
✔ Efficient storage and transmission

📌 Used in ZIP, JPEG, MP3

Prefix Codes
What is Prefix Code?

No code is prefix of another code

Avoids confusion during decoding

📌 Example:
A = 0
B = 10
C = 11

Huffman Coding Algorithm
Idea

Frequent characters → shorter codes

Less frequent → longer codes

Steps

Create leaf nodes for each character

Insert into priority queue

Remove two smallest frequency nodes

Merge them

Repeat until one node remains

Time Complexity

👉 O(n log n)

Space Complexity

👉 O(n)

