UNIT 5: DYNAMIC PROGRAMMING (8 Hrs)
What is Dynamic Programming (DP)?

Dynamic Programming is a method to solve problems by:
✔ Breaking a big problem into small overlapping sub-problems
✔ Solving each sub-problem only once
✔ Storing the result and reusing it

📌 DP avoids repeated work → faster algorithms

5.1 Greedy vs Dynamic Programming
Greedy Algorithm	Dynamic Programming
Makes local best choice	Finds global best solution
Faster, simpler	Slower but more accurate
No backtracking	Considers all possibilities
May fail	Always correct (if applied properly)

📌 Example:
Greedy fails in 0/1 Knapsack, DP works.

Recursion vs Dynamic Programming
Recursion	Dynamic Programming
Repeated calculations	Stores results
Slow	Fast
High time complexity	Optimized
No memory table	Uses DP table

📌 DP = Recursion + Memory

Elements of DP Strategy

Optimal Substructure
→ Optimal solution depends on optimal solutions of sub-problems

Overlapping Sub-problems
→ Same sub-problem solved many times

DP Table (Storage)
→ Store results of sub-problems

Bottom-Up / Top-Down approach

5.2 Dynamic Programming Algorithms
1️⃣ Matrix Chain Multiplication (MCM)
Problem

Given matrices, find best order of multiplication with minimum cost.

📌 Multiplication order matters, result does not.

Idea

Try all possible splits

Choose minimum cost

DP Formula
M[i][j] = min ( M[i][k] + M[k+1][j] + p[i−1]p[k]p[j] )

Time Complexity

👉 O(n³)

Space Complexity

👉 O(n²)

2️⃣ String Editing (Edit Distance)
Problem

Convert one string into another using:
✔ Insert
✔ Delete
✔ Replace

📌 Each operation has cost = 1

Example

Convert “CAT” → “CUT”
Replace A with U → cost = 1

DP Idea

Build table using smaller substrings

Compare characters

Time Complexity

👉 O(m × n)

Space Complexity

👉 O(m × n)

3️⃣ Zero-One Knapsack Problem
Problem

Items have weight and value

Knapsack has fixed capacity

Either take whole item or nothing

📌 Fractions NOT allowed

DP Idea
DP[i][w] = max value using first i items and capacity w

Time Complexity

👉 O(n × W)

Space Complexity

👉 O(n × W)

Why DP?

✔ Greedy fails
✔ DP gives optimal solution

4️⃣ Floyd-Warshall Algorithm
Problem

Find shortest paths between all pairs of vertices.

📌 Can handle negative edges, but no negative cycles

DP Idea

Gradually allow intermediate vertices

Update distances

Formula
dist[i][j] = min(dist[i][j], dist[i][k] + dist[k][j])

Time Complexity

👉 O(V³)

Space Complexity

👉 O(V²)

5️⃣ Travelling Salesman Problem (TSP)
Problem

Visit every city once

Return to starting city

Minimize total cost

📌 NP-Hard problem

DP Idea

Use bitmasking

Store visited cities

Time Complexity

👉 O(n² × 2ⁿ)

Space Complexity

👉 O(n × 2ⁿ)

5.3 Memoization Strategy
What is Memoization?

A top-down DP approach

Store results of recursive calls

Avoid recomputation

Example

Recursive Fibonacci with memoization:

Store fib(n)

Use stored value next time

Dynamic Programming vs Memoization
Dynamic Programming	Memoization
Bottom-up	Top-down
Iterative	Recursive
Table filled in order	Table filled on demand
No recursion	Uses recursion

📌 Both reduce time complexity

