UNIT 6: BACKTRACKING (5 Hrs)
What is Backtracking?

Backtracking is a technique where:
👉 We try one possible solution
👉 If it does not work, we go back (backtrack) and try another option

📌 It is like trial and error, but done systematically.

Real-life Example

Finding a way in a maze:

Go forward

If road is blocked → go back and choose another path

6.1 Concept of Backtracking

Backtracking:

Builds solution step by step

Checks constraints at each step

Stops early if solution is not possible

📌 It avoids unnecessary work by pruning bad choices.

General Backtracking Steps

Choose a candidate

Check if it is safe

Continue forward

If fails → undo choice (backtrack)

Recursion vs Backtracking
Recursion	Backtracking
Function calls itself	Uses recursion + undo
Solves all cases	Stops invalid paths
No checking of constraints	Checks constraints
Simple	More logical control

📌 Backtracking uses recursion, but recursion alone is not backtracking.

6.2 Backtracking Algorithms
1️⃣ Subset-Sum Problem
Problem

Given:

A set of numbers

A target sum

Find whether any subset adds up to the given sum.

📌 Example:
Set = {3, 4, 5}
Target = 9
Solution = {4, 5}

Idea

Include or exclude each element

Stop if sum becomes too large

Algorithm Idea
SubsetSum(i, currentSum)
if currentSum == target
    print solution
if i == n or currentSum > target
    return

include A[i]
SubsetSum(i+1, currentSum + A[i])

exclude A[i]
SubsetSum(i+1, currentSum)

Time Complexity

👉 O(2ⁿ) (worst case)

Space Complexity

👉 O(n) (recursion stack)

2️⃣ Zero-One Knapsack (Backtracking Approach)
Problem

Items have weight and profit

Capacity is limited

Either take item or leave it

📌 Different from DP version

Idea

Try all combinations

Prune paths when weight exceeds capacity

Algorithm Idea
Knapsack(i, profit, weight)
if weight ≤ capacity
    update maxProfit
if i == n
    return

include item i
Knapsack(i+1, profit + p[i], weight + w[i])

exclude item i
Knapsack(i+1, profit, weight)

Time Complexity

👉 O(2ⁿ)

Space Complexity

👉 O(n)

Note

✔ Backtracking is slower
✔ DP is better for large input

3️⃣ N-Queen Problem
Problem

Place N queens on an N×N chessboard such that:
✔ No two queens attack each other

📌 Queens attack horizontally, vertically, diagonally.

Idea

Place one queen in each row

Check column & diagonals

Backtrack if unsafe

Algorithm Idea
NQueen(row)
if row == N
    print solution
    return

for col = 1 to N
    if isSafe(row, col)
        place queen
        NQueen(row + 1)
        remove queen

Time Complexity

👉 O(N!) (worst case)

Space Complexity

👉 O(N)

📌 COMPARISON TABLE
Problem	Technique	Time
Subset Sum	Backtracking	2ⁿ
0/1 Knapsack	Backtracking	2ⁿ
N-Queen	Backtracking	N!
