7.1 Number Theoretic Notations
1️⃣ Divisibility

a | b → a divides b

a ∤ b → a does not divide b

2️⃣ Modulo Operation

a mod n = remainder when a is divided by n

📌 Example:
17 mod 5 = 2

3️⃣ Congruence
a ≡ b (mod n)


Means a and b give same remainder when divided by n.

📌 Example:
17 ≡ 2 (mod 5)

4️⃣ GCD (Greatest Common Divisor)

Largest number dividing both numbers.

📌 Example:
GCD(24, 36) = 12

5️⃣ Prime Number

A number greater than 1 with only two factors: 1 and itself.

Euclid’s Algorithm
Purpose

To find GCD of two numbers efficiently.

Idea

GCD(a, b) = GCD(b, a mod b)

Algorithm
Euclid(a, b)
while b ≠ 0
    r = a mod b
    a = b
    b = r
return a

Time Complexity

👉 O(log n)

Space Complexity

👉 O(1)

Extended Euclid’s Algorithm
Purpose

Finds:
✔ GCD(a, b)
✔ Values of x and y such that:

ax + by = gcd(a, b)


📌 Very important in cryptography.

Algorithm Idea

Same as Euclid

Also keeps track of x and y

Time Complexity

👉 O(log n)

Space Complexity

👉 O(log n) (recursive)

7.2 Solving Modular Linear Equations
Problem

Solve equation of the form:

ax ≡ b (mod n)

Condition for Solution

Solution exists iff:

gcd(a, n) | b

Solution Method

Compute g = gcd(a, n)

Divide equation by g

Use Extended Euclid to find inverse

Number of Solutions

👉 Exactly g solutions

Chinese Remainder Theorem (CRT)
Problem

Solve system of congruences:

x ≡ a₁ (mod n₁)
x ≡ a₂ (mod n₂)
...

Condition

All moduli must be pairwise coprime.

Idea

Combine smaller equations into one

Get a unique solution modulo product of moduli

Example
x ≡ 1 (mod 2)
x ≡ 2 (mod 3)


Solution:
x ≡ 5 (mod 6)

Time Complexity

👉 O(n log M)

Primality Testing
What is Primality Testing?

Check whether a number is prime or composite.

Miller–Rabin Randomized Primality Test
Purpose

Efficient test to check primality of very large numbers.

📌 Used in cryptography.

Idea (Simple)

Write n − 1 as 2^k × m

Pick random number a

Check modular exponentiation

Repeat test several times

Why Randomized?

Uses random values

Probability of error is very small

Result

“Probably Prime”

“Composite”

Time Complexity

👉 O(k log³ n)
(where k = number of tests)

Advantages

✔ Very fast
✔ Suitable for large numbers

📌 IMPORTANT COMPARISON TABLE
Algorithm	Purpose	Time
Euclid	GCD	log n
Extended Euclid	GCD + inverse	log n
CRT	Solve congruences	n log n
Miller-Rabin	Primality test	log³ n
