UNIT 8: NP-COMPLETENESS (5 Hrs)
Why NP-Completeness is Important

This unit answers one big question:

👉 Which problems can be solved efficiently and which cannot (so far)?

Used in:
✔ Algorithm design
✔ Complexity theory
✔ Real-world optimization problems

8.1 Tractable and Intractable Problems
Tractable Problems

Problems that can be solved efficiently.

📌 Solved in polynomial time
Examples:

Sorting

Searching

Shortest path (Dijkstra)

Intractable Problems

Problems that cannot be solved efficiently for large input.

📌 Take very long time as input grows
Examples:

Travelling Salesman Problem

Subset Sum

SAT

Polynomial Time Complexity

An algorithm runs in polynomial time if:

Time = O(n^k)   where k is constant


Examples:

O(n)

O(n²)

O(n³)

📌 Considered efficient and practical

Super-Polynomial Time Complexity

Time grows faster than any polynomial.

Examples:

O(2ⁿ)

O(n!)

O(nⁿ)

📌 Becomes impossible for large input

8.2 Complexity Classes
Class P
Definition

Problems that can be:
✔ Solved in polynomial time
✔ By a deterministic algorithm

📌 Example:

Sorting

Binary Search

Minimum Spanning Tree

Class NP
Definition

Problems for which:
✔ Given a solution, it can be verified in polynomial time

📌 Not necessarily solved fast, but checked fast

Example

SAT

Hamiltonian Path

Subset Sum

Class NP-Hard
Definition

Problems that are:
✔ At least as hard as NP problems
✔ Not necessarily in NP

📌 No known polynomial-time solution

Example

Halting Problem

Optimization versions of NP-Complete problems

Class NP-Complete
Definition

Problems that are:
✔ In NP
✔ NP-Hard

📌 Hardest problems in NP

Important Note

If any NP-Complete problem is solved in polynomial time:
👉 All NP problems can be solved in polynomial time

Relationship Diagram (Write in Exam)
P ⊆ NP ⊆ NP-Hard
     ∩
  NP-Complete

8.3 NP-Complete Problems
NP-Completeness

A problem is NP-Complete if:

It is in NP

Every NP problem can be reduced to it in polynomial time

Reducibility
What is Reduction?

Transforming problem A into problem B in polynomial time.

📌 If B is easy, A is also easy.

Cook’s Theorem
Statement

✔ Boolean Satisfiability Problem (SAT) is NP-Complete

📌 First NP-Complete problem

Importance

All other NP-Complete problems are proven using SAT.

Proof of NP-Completeness (Steps)

To prove a problem X is NP-Complete:

Show X ∈ NP

Choose known NP-Complete problem Y

Reduce Y → X in polynomial time

Examples of NP-Complete Problems
1️⃣ CNF-SAT

Boolean formula in Conjunctive Normal Form

Question: Is there a satisfying assignment?

✔ In NP
✔ NP-Complete (by Cook’s Theorem)

2️⃣ Vertex Cover
Problem

Given graph G and integer k, is there a set of ≤ k vertices that covers all edges?

✔ In NP
✔ NP-Complete

3️⃣ Subset Sum
Problem

Given set of numbers and sum S, does any subset add to S?

✔ In NP
✔ NP-Complete

8.4 Approximation Algorithms
What is an Approximation Algorithm?

Used when:
✔ Problem is NP-Complete
✔ Exact solution is too slow

👉 Gives near-optimal solution in polynomial time

Approximation for Vertex Cover
Idea

Pick an edge

Add both vertices to cover

Remove all incident edges

Repeat

Result

At most 2 times optimal solution

📌 2-approximation algorithm

Approximation for Subset Sum
Idea

Use greedy + trimming

Get sum close to target

📌 Not exact, but fast

IMPORTANT SUMMARY TABLE
Term	Meaning
P	Solvable in polynomial time
NP	Verifiable in polynomial time
NP-Hard	As hard as NP
NP-Complete	NP + NP-Hard
Reduction	Transform problem
Approximation	Near-optimal solution
